<main>

<div class="print-page" style="--pc: #06b6d4">
  <header class="print-header">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumbs">
      <a href="/gcsl">GCSL</a>
    </nav>
    <h1>Files and Workspaces</h1>
    <p class="print-note">Single-page view ¬∑ <a href="/gcsl/working-with-files/print.md">as markdown</a></p>
  </header>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="workspaces">Workspaces</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / Files and Workspaces</p>
    </header>
    <p>A GCSL workspace is a directory tree rooted at <code>workspace.gcsl</code>. Files in
one directory share public declarations. Child directories inherit the
public declarations above them, so common types and helpers can live at
the root without repeated imports.</p>
<p>The marker is an ordinary GCSL source file. It can be empty or declare
shared values and functions. The disk loader finds the nearest enclosing
marker when you run a file, reads the workspace, and checks every source
before executing the selected file and its dependencies.</p>
<h2 id="workspaces--public-and-private-names">Public and private names <a class="heading-anchor" href="#workspaces--public-and-private-names" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Fields, <code>def</code> functions, and declared types contribute to a directory's
public scope. <code>let</code>, <code>let mut</code>, and <code>fn</code> bindings remain private to their
file. Dotted public paths merge when they define distinct leaves; a
conflicting leaf is an error unless an earlier declaration explicitly
permits shadowing.</p>
<p>Sibling and child directories are not automatically in scope. Imports
name paths from the workspace root, with <code>/</code> between directories, as the
file system does. <code>import lib/helpers</code> names <code>lib/helpers.gcsl</code> or a
directory of that path. Use <code>as</code> for a local alias, or import a named
member with <code>from</code>.</p>
<p>A directory or file name in a path is written as it is, dots and all, so
<code>import pkgs/net.busybox.busybox as busybox</code> names the directory
<code>pkgs/net.busybox.busybox</code>. An import without <code>as</code> is named after the
path's last part, so that part must be a plain name; give a name such as
<code>net.busybox.busybox</code> one with <code>as</code>.</p>
<h2 id="workspaces--boundaries-and-evaluation-order">Boundaries and evaluation order <a class="heading-anchor" href="#workspaces--boundaries-and-evaluation-order" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Hidden names and nested directories containing their own <code>workspace.gcsl</code>
are excluded from an enclosing workspace's source discovery. A nested
workspace is independent, not an importable child module of the outer
workspace. Symbolic-link directory traversal is not a mechanism for
extending the source tree.</p>
<p>Dependencies determine file order; path order breaks ties. A file runs
as a whole before a file that depends on it. Cyclic file dependencies are
errors, including cycles introduced through function bodies or imports.</p>
<p>A standalone file outside any workspace can import standard modules but
cannot import arbitrary neighboring source files. Create a workspace
marker when several files need to cooperate.</p>
<h2 id="workspaces--deliberate-overrides">Deliberate overrides <a class="heading-anchor" href="#workspaces--deliberate-overrides" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>#[shadowable]</code> marks a public declaration or import as replaceable by a
later declaration in the workspace's defined order. The implicit prelude
import is shadowable, so your own declarations can use its names.</p>
<p>Use this attribute for intentional defaults. It is not an escape from
all conflicts: an ordinary public leaf still cannot be defined twice.
Whole-value overrides replace the old value, while distinct dotted
leaves can merge. See <code>gcsl(5)</code> for ordering and import syntax.</p>

  </section>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="organizing-a-workspace">Organizing a Workspace</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / Files and Workspaces</p>
    </header>
    <p>You can share a type and helper across files by putting them in a
workspace. This example gives the entry file access to root declarations
without an import.</p>
<h2 id="organizing-a-workspace--before-you-start">Before you start <a class="heading-anchor" href="#organizing-a-workspace--before-you-start" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>You need <code>gcsl</code> on your command search path and an empty writable directory.</p>
<h2 id="organizing-a-workspace--create-the-workspace">Create the workspace <a class="heading-anchor" href="#organizing-a-workspace--create-the-workspace" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p>Save these declarations as <code>workspace.gcsl</code>:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Component = {name: String, version: String}
def describe(item: Component): String {
    "{item.name} {item.version}"
}
</code></pre></div>
</li>
<li>
<p>Save the entry program as <code>main.gcsl</code> beside it:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>describe({name = "gcsl"; version = "0.1.0"})
</code></pre></div>
</li>
<li>
<p>From that directory, check the workspace and run the entry file:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="sh"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code><span class="hl-source hl-shell hl-bash"><span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-shell"><span class="hl-variable hl-function hl-shell">gcsl</span></span><span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-arguments hl-shell"> check</span>
<span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-shell"><span class="hl-variable hl-function hl-shell">gcsl</span></span><span class="hl-meta hl-function-call hl-arguments hl-shell"> run main.gcsl</span>
</span></code></pre></div>
</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="organizing-a-workspace--check-the-result">Check the result <a class="heading-anchor" href="#organizing-a-workspace--check-the-result" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>The check reports <code>no problems in 2 files checked</code>. The run prints
<code>gcsl 0.1.0</code>. The helper is visible because <code>def</code> contributes to the
shared directory scope. Replacing <code>def</code> with <code>fn</code> makes it private to
<code>workspace.gcsl</code>, so the entry file cannot resolve it.</p>
<p>When adding a child library directory, import its public fields or
functions by its root-relative path. Keep independent projects behind
their own workspace markers rather than relying on hidden files to
separate their scopes.</p>

  </section>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="file-and-process-effects">File and Process Effects</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / Files and Workspaces</p>
    </header>
    <p>GCSL provides standard functions for files and subprocesses. Their effects
use the host process's authority. Absolute paths and explicit command
arguments make those effects easier to inspect, but don't restrict what
a program can access.</p>
<h2 id="file-and-process-effects--locating-files">Locating files <a class="heading-anchor" href="#file-and-process-effects--locating-files" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Import <code>fileroot</code> to locate source directories and <code>path</code> to compose paths.
<code>fileroot.this</code> is the directory of the file containing the expression;
<code>fileroot.workspace</code> is the workspace root. <code>fileroot.caller</code> identifies
the source directory of the running function's caller.</p>
<p><code>path.join</code>, <code>path.normalize</code>, <code>path.dirname</code>, and <code>path.basename</code> operate
lexically. They don't resolve symbolic links or prove that a path stays
inside a directory. Use them for path construction, not access control.</p>
<p>File operations under <code>fs</code> require absolute paths. The following program
writes and reads a temporary file, then removes its temporary directory
when the run ends:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>import fs
import path
let directory = fs.temp_dir()
let file = path.join(directory, "message.txt")
fs.write(file, "GCSL")
fs.read(file)
</code></pre></div>
<p>Temporary directories last for the owning run, workspace, or REPL session.
Normal cleanup also runs after an evaluation error. Killing the process
can leave temporary directories behind. Atomic replacement by <code>fs.write</code>
protects readers from partial contents; it does not preserve a replaced
file's permissions or provide a durable transaction across several files.</p>
<h2 id="file-and-process-effects--running-a-process">Running a process <a class="heading-anchor" href="#file-and-process-effects--running-a-process" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>exec</code> accepts a list whose first element names the program. Later elements
are passed as arguments without shell interpretation. It returns a record
with the exit status and captured output. A nonzero exit status is data,
not a GCSL runtime error.</p>
<p><code>sh</code> instead executes a string through <code>/bin/sh</code> and fails the GCSL run
when the command fails. Don't insert untrusted text into that string.
Prefer <code>exec</code> when values need to become arguments rather than shell code.</p>
<p><code>exec_live</code> and <code>sh_live</code> also copy child output to GCSL's standard error
as lines arrive. They still capture output in memory. All these commands
inherit the host environment and working directory, and give the child
null standard input. A source-relative <code>fileroot</code> value does not change
a command's working directory.</p>
<p>Consult <code>gcsl-stdlib(3)</code> for exact function contracts and error behavior.</p>

  </section>
</div>

</main>

